s taking place before this  profound event of national mourning in  our nation's history it shouldn't have  happened  and it was wrong  that they have already apologized for  doing so  it was just plain wrong and it shouldn't  have happened but but the point i'm  making to you trevor is that we i think  in order to move on from this we first  of all need to establish the full facts  of what happened at all the different  uh alleged events that that took place  and that's the purpose of the sugray  inquiry the next step is then the prime  minister to come to the house of commons  to give a full response to be held to  account for that but i can tell you that  the prime minister is genuinely  committed  both in demonstrating his remorse and  apology for what happened but also in  taking steps to ensure that we address  the kind of culture in downing street  that enabled something like that to  happen which clearly should never have  happened in the first place  but i suppose what i'm really trying to  get at mr dowden is that i understand  the process and all of that  but  i i just wonder whether prime minister  or you really understand that that  process however clean it is  not going to answer  the question that is being asked out  there in the country and uh if you'll  forgive me i i've um  talked about this once before on this  program and i i hope never hoped never  to have to do it again but  uh  i think there are thousands millions of  people across the country who will feel  frankly rather as i do let's go back to  that april  17th 18th weekend and i just want to say  something about  what i did that during the week and i  think other people have exactly the same  story that week  i saw my two daughters at various times  outside  one was pregnant one was very ill their  mother stepmother and i weren't allowed  to meet them together  we all stuck to the spirit and the  letter the rules  on the saturday after watching the  funeral of prince philip i went to one 